    "---\n",
    "## 7. Manual Multinomial Naive Bayes Implementation\n",
    "\n",
    "### 7.1 Mathematical Formulation\n",
    "Let $\\mathcal{C} = \\{c_1, c_2, \\dots, c_K\\}$ be the set of class labels, and let $\\mathbf{x} = [x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_{|\\mathcal{V}|}]^T$ be the Bag-of-Words count vector of a document $d$.\n",
    "\n",
    "By Bayes' Theorem:\n",
    "$$P(c \\mid \\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{P(c) P(\\mathbf{x} \\mid c)}{P(\\mathbf{x})} \\propto P(c) \\prod_{j=1}^{|\\mathcal{V}|} P(w_j \\mid c)^{x_j}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 1. Prior Probabilities\n",
    "The prior probability of class $c$ is estimated via Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE):\n",
    "$$P(c) = \\frac{N_c}{N_{total}}$$\n",
    "In log-space:\n",
    "$$\\log P(c) = \\log(N_c) - \\log(N_{total})$$\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 2. Likelihood with Laplace (Add-1) Smoothing\n",
    "To prevent zero-probability multiplication when an unseen word is encountered during testing, we apply additive Laplace smoothing ($\\alpha = 1.0$):\n",
    "$$P(w_j \\mid c) = \\frac{\\text{count}(w_j, c) + \\alpha}{\\sum_{k=1}^{|\\mathcal{V}|} \\text{count}(w_k, c) + \\alpha \\cdot |\\mathcal{V}|}$$\n",
    "In log-space:\n",
    "$$\\log P(w_j \\mid c) = \\log(\\text{count}(w_j, c) + \\alpha) - \\log\\left(\\sum_{k=1}^{|\\mathcal{V}|} \\text{count}(w_k, c) + \\alpha \\cdot |\\mathcal{V}|\\right)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 3. Log-Space Joint Likelihood & Classification Decision\n",
    "To eliminate arithmetic underflow caused by multiplying numerous small probabilities:\n",
    "$$\\log P(c, \\mathbf{x}) = \\log P(c) + \\sum_{j=1}^{|\\mathcal{V}|} x_j \\cdot \\log P(w_j \\mid c)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Decision rule:\n",
    "$$\\hat{c} = \\arg\\max_{c \\in \\mathcal{C}} \\log P(c, \\mathbf{x})$$\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 4. Normalized Posterior Probabilities via Log-Sum-Exp\n",
    "To calculate normalized posterior log-probabilities:\n",
    "$$\\log P(c \\mid \\mathbf{x}) = \\log P(c, \\mathbf{x}) - \\text{logsumexp}_{c' \\in \\mathcal{C}}(\\log P(c', \\mathbf{x}))$$\n",
    "where $\\text{logsumexp}(\\mathbf{z}) = m + \\log \\sum_k \\exp(z_k - m)$ with $m = \\max(\\mathbf{z})$ to preserve numerical stability."
   ]
  },
